            <ArticleTitle>[To Test Glutamate Hypothesis for Schizophrenia Utilizing Proton Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1035-1040</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.11477/mf.1416200863</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recent advancement in magnetic resonance spectroscopy (MRS) has elucidated the pathophysiology of mental illness, including schizophrenia. MRS is a neuroimaging technique that non-invasively measures chemicals, using nuclear magnetic resonance. This narrative review explains proton MRS (&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;H-MRS) and introduces pivotal studies to examine a glutamate hypothesis for schizophrenia, employing &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;H-MRS.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
